## What I changed

The task is Prefab's "update many aggregates from one event" pattern, so I used `@Multicast` + a `@RepositoryMixin` — no hand-written listeners, services, or migrations.

**1. `src/main/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatchRepositoryMixin.java` (new)** — the query that finds every watch of a product:
```java
@RepositoryMixin(PriceWatch.class)
public interface PriceWatchRepositoryMixin {
    List<PriceWatch> findByProduct(Reference<Product> product);
}
```

**2. `PriceWatch.java`** — added the multicast handler on the aggregate:
```java
@EventHandler
@Multicast(queryMethod = "findByProduct", parameters = "product", failWhenEmpty = false)
public PriceWatch onPriceChanged(PriceChanged event) {
    return new PriceWatch(id, version, email, product, event.price());
}
```
`parameters = "product"` maps the event's `product` field onto the query; the handler is run once per matching watch, so **all** watches of that product get the new price, and watches of other products are never queried. `failWhenEmpty = false` is the key for "no watches → succeed": the generated `PriceWatchService.onPriceChanged` does `if (aggregates.isEmpty()) return;`, so a product with no watches returns cleanly instead of throwing — no retry, no dead-letter.

One subtlety worth flagging: `@EventHandler`'s `value()` is a *merge-target aggregate*, not the event. Passing `@EventHandler(PriceChanged.class)` made the processor demand that `PriceChanged` be `@Aggregate` (a compiler error). The event type is derived from the handler's parameter, so the annotation is left bare here.

**3. `src/test/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatchIntegrationTest.java` (new)** — an `@IntegrationTest` that drives it through HTTP: a price change updates the matching watch (asserted via Awaitility, since in-memory delivery is async), leaves a watch for a *different* product untouched, and confirms a price change on a product with no watches still succeeds.

## Verification
`mvn verify` → **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the generated `PriceChangedEventConformanceTest` plus the two new integration tests, all running in-memory without Docker). The generated `target/prefab/agent-context.md` confirms `PriceWatch` now "Handles: `PriceChanged` via `onPriceChanged()` on topic `prices`".

Done. From a clean `mvn clean verify`, the build is green (3/3 tests) and the generated `OrderRepository` now exposes both new methods on the single repository.

## What I did

**Added `src/main/java/com/example/ordering/application/OrderReportingQueries.java`** — a Prefab `@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)` interface declaring the two derived queries:

```java
@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)
public interface OrderReportingQueries {
    List<Order> findByCustomerEmail(String customerEmail);
    long countByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

This is the Prefab-idiomatic way to add queries to an aggregate's repository without hand-writing a `@Repository`. Prefab's annotation processor adds this interface as a **superinterface** of the generated `com.example.ordering.application.OrderRepository`, so its methods land on that one repository (verified: `public interface OrderRepository extends CrudRepository<Order, String>, PagingAndSortingRepository<Order, String>, OrderReportingQueries`).

## Key design choice

The mixin is a **plain interface** — it deliberately does *not* extend a Spring Data `Repository` base. If it did, Spring Data's scanner could register it as a *separate* repository bean, which is exactly the "second repository for orders" the task forbids. Keeping it plain means it contributes only method signatures to the single generated repository.

## Verification

I added a focused `@IntegrationTest` (`OrderReportingQueriesTest`) that:
- `findByCustomerEmail` returns all of a customer's orders;
- `countByStatus` returns the correct per-status count;
- `applicationContext.getBeansOfType(OrderRepository.class)` has **exactly one** bean — proving no second repository was created.

All three pass in-memory (no Docker) under `mvn clean verify`, which also runs the generated conformance wiring. I did not touch anything under `target/`; the repository change flows entirely through the mixin and the recompile.

`findByCustomerEmail` maps to the `customer_email` column and `countByStatus` binds the `OrderStatus` enum to its `VARCHAR` column — both confirmed by the passing tests.

All requirements are met and the full build passes. Here's what I did.

## What I added

A new `Task` aggregate in `com.example.planning` (`src/main/java/com/example/planning/Task.java`), modeled as a child of `Project` via Prefab's `@Parent` rather than a hand-written controller/service/repository:

```java
@Aggregate
@GetById
@GetList
public record Task(
        @Id Reference<Task> id,
        @Version long version,
        @Parent Reference<Project> project,
        @NotNull String title,
        boolean done
) {
    @Create
    public Task(Reference<Project> project, @NotNull String title) {
        this(Reference.create(), 0L, project, title, false);   // done starts false
    }

    @Update(path = "/complete", method = "POST")
    public Task complete() {
        return new Task(id, version, project, title, true);
    }
}
```

Prefab generated the REST wiring, and `target/prefab/agent-context.md` confirms the four endpoints come out exactly as specified:

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `POST /projects/{projectId}/tasks` | `201 Created`, `Location: /projects/{projectId}/tasks/{id}` (parent injected from the path var; body is just `{"title":...}`) |
| `GET /projects/{projectId}/tasks/{id}` | `TaskResponse` with `id`, `title`, `done` (+`version`,`project`) |
| `GET /projects/{projectId}/tasks` | `PagedModel` (Spring Data page, `content` array), scoped to the project via `findByProject` |
| `POST /projects/{projectId}/tasks/{id}/complete` | `200 OK`, sets `done = true` |

Each list/get path also guards with `belongsToParent(projectId, id)`, so a task can only be read/completed under its own project.

## Verification

- `mvn -DskipTests compile` and `mvn verify` both **BUILD SUCCESS**.
- I added `src/test/java/com/example/planning/TaskIntegrationTest.java` (`@IntegrationTest`, using the generated `ProjectClient`/`TaskClient`) covering: create returns an id, get-by-id returns title + `done=false`, the list holds the task and excludes tasks of a *different* project, and `complete` flips `done` to `true`. It passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).

No Prefab security or event conformance tests are generated here (no security module on the classpath and no `@Event` types), so the only test in the suite is the one above. I kept everything within Prefab's annotations — no hand-written controllers, entities, repositories, or migrations.

The task is complete. `mvn verify` passes with **BUILD SUCCESS** (6 tests, 0 failures).

## What I changed

**`src/main/java/com/example/crm/Customer.java`** — added Jakarta validation to the `@Create` constructor parameters (the cheat sheet is explicit that validation goes on the operation parameters, not the record components):

```java
@Create
public Customer(@NotBlank @Size(max = 100) String name, @NotNull @Email String email) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, name, email);
}
```

- `name`: `@NotBlank` (required, non-blank) + `@Size(max = 100)` (at most 100 chars)
- `email`: `@NotNull` (required) + `@Email` (valid e-mail address)

Prefab propagated these into the generated `CreateCustomerRequest`, and the generated `CustomerController`/`CustomerService` already apply `@Valid`, so invalid bodies are rejected with `400` before `save()` — nothing is persisted.

## Verification

I added **`src/test/java/com/example/crm/CustomerValidationIntegrationTest.java`** (`@IntegrationTest`, in-memory, no Docker) covering:
- valid request → `201` and a row stored (`count` +1);
- blank / missing / 101-char `name` → `400` and no row stored;
- missing / malformed `email` → `400` and no row stored.

Each negative case asserts the repository count is unchanged, proving nothing is stored on rejection. Counts are compared as deltas so the tests are order-independent.

Build loop used, per the cheat sheet: `mvn -DskipTests compile` → `mvn -Dtest=CustomerValidationIntegrationTest test` → `mvn verify`.

No hand-written controllers, entities, migrations, or DTOs were added — the change is purely annotations on the record, and everything else stays generated.
